      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exploring Bell's Theorem in greater depth requires an appreciation of the mathematical underpinning and the philosophical implications of its violation, as seen in actual experiments. Let's begin with the local realistic perspective, where phenomena are determined by pre-existing properties and signals do not exceed the speed of light.\n\nBell's inequality dissects these ideas using mathematics. Starting with a pair of entangled particles, their respective spin measurements at two different locations, A and B, are analyzed. At each site, we measure the spin along different axes, denoted by angles θ_A and θ_B respectively. The observable quantities are the correlations between these measurements, which form the basis of Bell's inequality, stating that:\n\n|E(θ_A, θ_B) - E(θ_A, θ_B')| + |E(θ_A', θ_B) + E(θ_A', θ_B')| ≤ 2\n\nHere, E represents the expectation value of the product of spins measured by two observers.\n\nViolation of this inequality by quantum mechanics can be shown intuitively by considering the predictions for perfect anti-correlated and perfectly correlated spins and their compromises. Quantum mechanics predicts a sine-squared relation that, at certain angles, exceeds the upper bound set by Bell's inequality,",
      "source": "target",
